Scout — the agent you’ll build from scratch in Module 03 — doesn’t have its loop yet. But its system prompt is code, and you can start engineering it now. Work in a scout/ package and log everything you find to notes.md. (a) Versioned, parameterized prompt with runtime facts at the end . Create scout/prompts.py. Put the stable system prompt in a module-level constant, and inject runtime facts (date, cwd) via a template at the end so the stable prefix can be prompt-cached (forward-ref Module 10): . # scout/prompts.py SCOUT_SYSTEM = """\ You are Scout, a coding agent that works in a user's repository. Capabilities &amp; constraints: - You read and edit files and run commands through tools (defined separately). - You work only inside the user's working directory. - For minor, reversible choices, act and note it. For destructive actions, confirm first. Output: - Lead with the outcome. Keep narration between actions to one line. """ def render_system(*, date: str, cwd: str) -&gt; str: # Runtime facts LAST → stable prefix stays byte-identical → cacheable. return f"{SCOUT_SYSTEM}
&lt;env&gt;
Date: {date}
Working directory: {cwd}
&lt;/env&gt;" . The key discipline: the only thing that varies between requests is the trailing &lt;env&gt; block. Everything above it is frozen. (If you interpolate the date into the header, you invalidate the cache on every call — that’s the cache bug in “Real production issues” below.) . (b) Deliberate prompt variants, run N times on the same tasks . Pick a small fixed set of tasks (3–5 is plenty — e.g. “summarize what this module does,” “rename this function across the file,” “is this diff safe to merge?”). Write 2–3 deliberate variants of the task prompt and run each variant N times (say N=5) on every task. Compare: . | terse vs. verbose instruction | more-autonomous vs. ask-first | with vs. without a worked example | . Record, per variant: output length, whether it asked vs. acted, whether it over- or under-used the (stubbed) tools, and your quality read. The point isn’t a winner — it’s seeing the spread, and feeling how a one-word prompt change moves the distribution. (c) Demonstrate the “dial back CRITICAL” lesson . Take a task where a tool should fire only sometimes. Write an over-aggressive instruction (CRITICAL: YOU MUST ALWAYS USE THE search TOOL...) and confirm it over-triggers across your N runs. Then soften it (“Use search when the answer depends on current info you don’t have…”) and confirm the behavior normalizes. Log the before/after trigger rates. This is the most important muscle in the module. (d) Convert a prose JSON request into structured output . Take a task where Scout returns structured data (e.g. a list of findings). First, ask for it in prose: “Return JSON like [{"line": 1, "severity": "high", "message": "..."}].” Run it N times and count malformed outputs (wrong keys, prose preamble, trailing commentary). Then convert to a messages.parse(..., output_format=...) call with a Pydantic model and rerun. Note the reliability difference in notes.md — this is “move correctness to the boundary” made concrete. (e) Stretch: stub an eval harness hook . You’ll build the real eval suite in Module 08. For now, stub the interface so prompt changes become measurable instead of vibe-based: . # scout/eval_stub.py def score(task: str, output: str) -&gt; dict: """Placeholder scorer. Module 08 replaces this with real evals.""" return { "asked_permission": "?" in output and "should i" in output.lower(), "length_chars": len(output), "mentions_tool": "search" in output.lower(), } . Wire your variant runner from (b) through score() so each run produces a row of numbers. The numbers are crude — that’s fine. The point is that your harness now emits a measurement per prompt change, which is the seam Module 08 plugs into. From here on, a prompt edit without a number next to it is a smell. ✅ Self-check for the exercise: Could you hand scout/prompts.py and your variant table to a teammate and have them reproduce your conclusion about which variant to ship? If not, you’re still iterating on vibes.

