This is the centerpiece. You’ll build a reusable harness in evals/, use it to make a real ship/no-ship call, calibrate the judge, and mine a failure. Assume Scout from Module 03 is importable as scout.loop.run_agent(task, cwd) -&gt; Transcript, where Transcript carries .final_text, .messages, and .usage (tokens). Adapt the import to your code. Layout . evals/ cases/ # frozen fixtures + grading keys bugfix_off_by_one/ fixture/ # the pristine world: copied fresh per trial calc.py test_calc.py case.yaml # task, slice tags, grader spec extract_invoice/ fixture/ invoice.txt case.yaml summarize_incident/ fixture/ incident.md case.yaml ... # 8–12 total, mixing grader types harness.py # runner: copy fixture, run Scout k times, grade, record graders.py # programmatic + assertion-list + LLM-judge stats.py # Wilson interval + aggregation runs/ # output: per-trial transcripts, scores, op metrics . (a) Author the dataset (8–12 cases, mixed graders) . Each case is a directory: a fixture/ (the frozen world) and a case.yaml (task + tags + grader). Mix the grader families so you exercise the whole spectrum. A representative spread: . # evals/cases/bugfix_off_by_one/case.yaml id: bugfix_off_by_one slice: bugfix difficulty: easy task: "There is a failing test in this directory. Find and fix the bug so all tests pass. Do not edit the tests." grader: type: programmatic check: pytest # green test suite == pass. The gold standard. # evals/cases/extract_invoice/case.yaml id: extract_invoice slice: extraction difficulty: medium task: "Read invoice.txt and write invoice.json containing keys: invoice_number, total_amount (number), due_date (YYYY-MM-DD)." grader: type: assertions # must / must_not predicates over the output file file: invoice.json must_be_valid_json: true must_contain: ["invoice_number", "total_amount", "due_date"] expect_json: invoice_number: "INV-4471" total_amount: 1840.00 . # evals/cases/summarize_incident/case.yaml id: summarize_incident slice: summary difficulty: hard task: "Read incident.md and write a 3-sentence summary to summary.txt covering: the root cause, the customer impact, and the fix." grader: type: judge # genuinely fuzzy quality -&gt; LLM-as-judge with a rubric file: summary.txt # the agent's output, read from the mutated fixture copy source: incident.md # the ground-truth source, read from the pristine fixture rubric: | Score 1-5. A 5 names the ROOT CAUSE (the database connection pool exhaustion), states the CUSTOMER IMPACT (checkout failures for ~12 minutes), and the FIX (raised pool size + added a circuit breaker). Deduct for hallucinated facts not in the source, for missing any of the three required elements, or for padding beyond 3 sentences. A 5 is concise, accurate, and complete. pass_threshold: 4 . Round out to 8–12 with more bugfix cases (varying difficulty), a JSON-validity extraction, a knowledge-base QA case graded by assertions (must_contain the correct fact, must_not contain a known wrong answer), and at least one adversarial case (an empty file, a contradictory instruction) to probe the tail. The mix is deliberate: most cases programmatic/assertion (cheap, trustworthy), a few judge cases for the genuinely fuzzy ones. (b) The graders . # evals/graders.py import json, subprocess from pathlib import Path from pydantic import BaseModel, Field from anthropic import Anthropic client = Anthropic() JUDGE_MODEL = "claude-opus-4-8" # strong judge, even though Scout runs on Sonnet # --- 1. Programmatic: run the test suite in the (mutated) fixture copy -------- def grade_pytest(workdir: Path) -&gt; tuple[bool, str]: r = subprocess.run(["pytest", "-q"], cwd=workdir, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120) return r.returncode == 0, r.stdout[-2000:] + r.stderr[-2000:] # --- 2. Assertion list: must / must_not predicates over an output file -------- def grade_assertions(workdir: Path, spec: dict) -&gt; tuple[bool, str]: target = workdir / spec["file"] if not target.exists(): return False, f"expected output file {spec['file']} was never created" text = target.read_text() notes = [] if spec.get("must_be_valid_json"): try: data = json.loads(text) except json.JSONDecodeError as e: return False, f"invalid JSON: {e}" for k, v in spec.get("expect_json", {}).items(): if data.get(k) != v: notes.append(f"field {k}: expected {v!r}, got {data.get(k)!r}") for needle in spec.get("must_contain", []): if needle not in text: notes.append(f"missing required substring: {needle!r}") for needle in spec.get("must_not_contain", []): if needle in text: notes.append(f"contains forbidden substring: {needle!r}") return (len(notes) == 0), ("; ".join(notes) or "all assertions passed") # --- 3. LLM-as-judge: structured verdict against a rubric --------------------- class Verdict(BaseModel): evidence: list[str] = Field(description="Exact quotes from the output that justify the score. Cite BEFORE scoring.") reasoning: str = Field(description="How the evidence maps to the rubric.") score: int = Field(ge=1, le=5, description="1-5 per the rubric.") JUDGE_SYSTEM = ( "You are a strict, fair grader. Apply the rubric literally. " "First quote the exact spans of the OUTPUT that bear on the rubric, then reason, then score. " "Judge only against the rubric and the SOURCE — never reward length or style. " "If a required element is absent from the output, it is missing even if it is 'obvious'." ) def grade_judge(workdir: Path, spec: dict, source_text: str) -&gt; tuple[bool, str, int]: target = workdir / spec["file"] output = target.read_text() if target.exists() else "(no output file was produced)" resp = client.messages.parse( model=JUDGE_MODEL, max_tokens=1024, system=JUDGE_SYSTEM, messages=[{"role": "user", "content": f"RUBRIC:
{spec['rubric']}

SOURCE:
{source_text}

OUTPUT TO GRADE:
{output}"}], output_format=Verdict, ) v: Verdict = resp.parsed_output passed = v.score &gt;= spec["pass_threshold"] return passed, f"score={v.score} reason={v.reasoning} evidence={v.evidence}", v.score . Note the structured judge: evidence is required and ordered first so the model must cite before it scores; the verdict is a typed Verdict, never free prose; the system prompt explicitly names and disarms the length and style biases. (c) The runner — fresh fixture per trial, k runs, parallel, full recording . # evals/harness.py import shutil, tempfile, json, yaml, time from pathlib import Path from concurrent.futures import ThreadPoolExecutor from scout.loop import run_agent # adapt to your Scout entrypoint from graders import grade_pytest, grade_assertions, grade_judge CASES = Path(__file__).parent / "cases" RUNS = Path(__file__).parent / "runs" K = 3 # runs per case (full suite). Smoke suite sets K=1. MAX_CONCURRENCY = 8 # respect API rate limits def load_cases(slice_filter=None): cases = [] for d in sorted(CASES.iterdir()): if not (d / "case.yaml").exists(): continue spec = yaml.safe_load((d / "case.yaml").read_text()) if slice_filter and spec["slice"] != slice_filter: continue cases.append((d, spec)) return cases def run_trial(case_dir: Path, spec: dict, trial_idx: int) -&gt; dict: # Isolated, reproducible: copy the frozen fixture into a fresh temp dir. work = Path(tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix=f"{spec['id']}_{trial_idx}_")) shutil.copytree(case_dir / "fixture", work, dirs_exist_ok=True) t0 = time.time() transcript = run_agent(task=spec["task"], cwd=str(work)) # Scout acts on the COPY latency = time.time() - t0 g = spec["grader"]; score_num = None if g["type"] == "programmatic" and g["check"] == "pytest": passed, detail = grade_pytest(work) elif g["type"] == "assertions": passed, detail = grade_assertions(work, g) elif g["type"] == "judge": # Read the source the judge grades against from the PRISTINE fixture # (the case names the source file under grader.source). source = (case_dir / "fixture" / g["source"]).read_text() passed, detail, score_num = grade_judge(work, g, source) else: raise ValueError(f"unknown grader {g['type']}") rec = { "case": spec["id"], "slice": spec["slice"], "trial": trial_idx, "passed": bool(passed), "score": score_num, "detail": detail, "latency_s": round(latency, 2), "tokens": getattr(transcript, "usage", None) and { "in": transcript.usage.input_tokens, "out": transcript.usage.output_tokens}, "steps": sum(1 for m in transcript.messages if m["role"] == "assistant"), } # Record the FULL transcript (the trace) + score + op metrics. out = RUNS / spec["id"] / f"trial_{trial_idx}" out.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True) (out / "record.json").write_text(json.dumps(rec, indent=2, default=str)) (out / "transcript.json").write_text(json.dumps(transcript.messages, indent=2, default=str)) shutil.rmtree(work, ignore_errors=True) # clean up the copy return rec def run_suite(slice_filter=None, k=K) -&gt; list[dict]: jobs = [(d, spec, i) for (d, spec) in load_cases(slice_filter) for i in range(k)] with ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=MAX_CONCURRENCY) as ex: return list(ex.map(lambda a: run_trial(*a), jobs)) . (d) Aggregate with confidence intervals . # evals/stats.py import math from collections import defaultdict def wilson(successes: int, n: int, z: float = 1.96) -&gt; tuple[float, float, float]: """Wilson score interval for a binomial proportion. Returns (rate, low, high).""" if n == 0: return 0.0, 0.0, 0.0 p = successes / n denom = 1 + z**2 / n center = (p + z**2 / (2 * n)) / denom half = (z * math.sqrt(p * (1 - p) / n + z**2 / (4 * n**2))) / denom return p, max(0.0, center - half), min(1.0, center + half) def aggregate(records: list[dict]) -&gt; dict: # pass rate per slice, with Wilson CI — the headline numbers. by_slice = defaultdict(lambda: [0, 0]) # slice -&gt; [successes, n] by_case = defaultdict(list) # case -&gt; [bool, ...] (for pass^k / pass@k) for r in records: s = by_slice[r["slice"]]; s[1] += 1; s[0] += int(r["passed"]) by_case[r["case"]].append(r["passed"]) report = {"slices": {}, "reliability": {}} for sl, (succ, n) in by_slice.items(): rate, lo, hi = wilson(succ, n) report["slices"][sl] = {"rate": round(rate, 3), "ci": [round(lo, 3), round(hi, 3)], "n": n} for case, results in by_case.items(): report["reliability"][case] = { "pass_at_k": any(results), # &gt;=1 success: salvageable-with-retry "pass_pow_k": all(results), # ALL succeed: the autonomy metric "consistency": round(sum(results) / len(results), 2), } return report . Print it and you get exactly the artifact §3c demanded — per-slice rates with intervals, plus per-case pass^k: . slice=bugfix rate=0.83 ci=[0.59, 0.94] n=18 &lt;- wide interval: only 18 trials slice=extraction rate=0.94 ci=[0.73, 0.99] n=12 slice=summary rate=0.67 ci=[0.39, 0.86] n=9 reliability bugfix_off_by_one pass^k=True consistency=1.00 reliability summarize_incident pass^k=False consistency=0.67 &lt;- inconsistent: autonomy risk . (e) Use it for real — a ship/no-ship decision . Now do the thing the whole harness exists for. Record the current runs/ as your baseline. Then make a real change to Scout’s system prompt — say, add “Be extremely concise; prefer the shortest correct answer.” Re-run the full suite and compare per slice: . base = aggregate(load_records("runs_baseline")) cand = aggregate(run_suite()) for sl in base["slices"]: b, c = base["slices"][sl], cand["slices"][sl] # Regression rule: candidate's rate dropped BELOW the baseline's lower CI bound. regressed = c["rate"] &lt; b["ci"][0] print(f"{sl:12} base={b['rate']:.2f}{b['ci']} cand={c['rate']:.2f}{c['ci']} " f"{'❌ REGRESSION' if regressed else 'ok'}") . Make the call. If no slice dropped below its baseline lower bound, ship — the change is at worst neutral. If summary rose (concise summaries score better on the rubric) but bugfix dropped beyond its interval (the agent got too terse and stopped explaining its reasoning, hurting multi-step debugging) — no-ship, and you’ve learned something real about that prompt change that you’d never have seen in a demo. This is the entire point: a prompt edit is no longer a vibe, it’s a measured per-slice diff. (f) Calibrate the judge . Before you trust the summary slice, prove the judge isn’t lying. Hand-grade 5 summary outputs yourself (1–5), then have the judge grade the same 5, and measure agreement: . human = {"t0": 5, "t1": 3, "t2": 4, "t3": 2, "t4": 4} # your labels judge = {t: grade_judge(...)[2] for t in human} # judge's scores exact = sum(human[t] == judge[t] for t in human) / len(human) within1 = sum(abs(human[t] - judge[t]) &lt;= 1 for t in human) / len(human) print(f"exact agreement={exact:.0%} within-1={within1:.0%}") . If within-1 is high (≈100%) and exact agreement is decent, the judge is usable — trust the summary slice. If they diverge, your judge is the problem, not Scout: tighten the rubric (more concrete anchors per score), add the disarm-the-bias instructions, or switch judge models, and re-calibrate. Never trust a judge slice you haven’t calibrated. (g) Mine a failure into a regression case . Find a case Scout fails (or induce one — feed it a gnarly real task it bungles). Freeze that exact situation: snapshot the input files into a new cases/&lt;name&gt;/fixture/, write a case.yaml whose grading key encodes the correct outcome, and confirm the case currently fails. It is now a permanent regression test — the flywheel in miniature. Wire run_suite(slice_filter="smoke") with k=1 into your CI on every push, and the full suite into a nightly job. You now have a reusable eval harness with a smoke gate, a full suite, calibrated judging, and a flywheel. That harness is worth more than any single prompt improvement, because it makes every future improvement measurable.

