This exercise is deliberately lighter than other modules’ — customization is infrastructure-heavy, and the real learning here is judgment, not pipeline-plumbing. Do part (a) carefully (it’s the whole point), run the small demo in (b), and sketch (c). (a) Decision drill — the core of this exercise . For each problem, decide: prompt / few-shot / RAG / tools / fine-tune (and if fine-tune, which method). Write one sentence of justification before reading the answer. | “Our agent must answer questions from our internal wiki, which is updated daily.” | “Our agent must output our exact ticket-JSON schema, every single time, with no drift.” | “We run 5M cheap text-classification calls per day and want frontier-model quality at Haiku cost.” | . Answers 1. **RAG (Module 05) — not fine-tuning.** The gap is *knowledge*, and it *changes daily*. Fine-tuning can't add it and can't keep it current; you'd be retraining every day and still hallucinating. Index the wiki, retrieve, and cite. This is the canonical wrong-tool trap. 2. **Structured outputs first; SFT only if that's not enough.** The gap is *format consistency*. Try the cheapest lever — structured-output / JSON-schema enforcement (Module 04/08) — which guarantees the shape without touching weights. If you genuinely need behavior beyond what schema enforcement covers (e.g. consistent *content* choices within the schema), SFT on a few hundred clean examples is the next rung. Note: format/style is exactly what SFT is *good* at. 3. **Distillation.** This is the textbook case: narrow task, enormous volume, cost/latency-sensitive. Generate high-quality labels with `claude-opus-4-8`, then either few-shot `claude-haiku-4-5` or fine-tune a small model on those labels. Verify with your eval harness that the cheap model is close enough, and the cost delta pays for the whole project many times over. (First confirm prompt caching + tiering alone don't already get you there.) The three answers trace the ladder: **knowledge → RAG, format → structured-output/SFT, cost-at-scale → distillation.** (b) Distillation mini-demo . Pick one narrow task (sentiment of support messages, intent classification, short-text categorization — anything with a checkable answer). Then: . Step 1 — Generate ~50 high-quality labels with a strong model. import anthropic client = anthropic.Anthropic() STRONG_MODEL = "claude-opus-4-8" # or "claude-fable-5" for the hardest tasks # Your raw, unlabeled inputs for the narrow task. inputs = [ "The new dashboard is gorgeous but it logs me out every 10 minutes.", "Cancel my subscription. This is the third time I've asked.", "Works fine, no complaints.", # ... ~50 total ] LABELS = ["positive", "negative", "neutral", "mixed"] def strong_label(text: str) -&gt; str: resp = client.messages.create( model=STRONG_MODEL, max_tokens=16000, thinking={"type": "adaptive"}, output_config={ "format": { "type": "json_schema", "schema": { "type": "object", "properties": {"label": {"type": "string", "enum": LABELS}}, "required": ["label"], "additionalProperties": False, }, } }, messages=[{"role": "user", "content": f"Classify the sentiment of this support message.

{text}"}], ) import json text_block = next(b.text for b in resp.content if b.type == "text") return json.loads(text_block)["label"] # This labeled set is your distillation dataset (and a held-out eval slice). dataset = [{"text": t, "label": strong_label(t)} for t in inputs] . Step 2 — Approximate it cheaply. Two options: . Option A — few-shot a cheap model (no training infra needed): pick your 5–8 clearest labeled examples as few-shot exemplars and run claude-haiku-4-5 against them. CHEAP_MODEL = "claude-haiku-4-5" few_shot = dataset[:6] # use your cleanest, most representative examples exemplars = "
".join(f"Message: {d['text']}
Label: {d['label']}" for d in few_shot) def cheap_label(text: str) -&gt; str: resp = client.messages.create( model=CHEAP_MODEL, max_tokens=256, messages=[{"role": "user", "content": f"Classify sentiment as one of {LABELS}.

{exemplars}

Message: {text}
Label:"}], ) out = next(b.text for b in resp.content if b.type == "text").strip().lower() return next((l for l in LABELS if l in out), "neutral") . Option B — fine-tune a small model (if you have access). Using a provider with a public fine-tuning API — OpenAI is the concrete example here — the flow is conceptually: write your dataset to JSONL as (prompt, completion) / chat pairs, upload it, and kick off a job: . # Provider-honest note: OpenAI exposes a public fine-tuning API (and reinforcement # fine-tuning). Anthropic's general guidance leans toward prompting + context + tools # over fine-tuning for most cases; some fine-tuning is available via cloud platforms — # check provider docs rather than assuming a first-party endpoint. from openai import OpenAI oai = OpenAI() # 1. Upload your distillation dataset as JSONL (chat-format examples). training_file = oai.files.create( file=open("distill_dataset.jsonl", "rb"), purpose="fine-tune", ) # 2. Launch the fine-tuning job on a small base model. job = oai.fine_tuning.jobs.create( training_file=training_file.id, model="gpt-4.1-mini", # a small/cheap base — the distillation target ) # 3. Poll job status; when it succeeds, you get a fine-tuned model ID to serve. Step 3 — Measure with your Module 08 eval harness. Hold out a labeled slice the cheap model never saw, and compare cheap-vs-strong on both quality and cost/latency: . holdout = dataset[40:] # never used as few-shot / training def accuracy(predict_fn): correct = sum(predict_fn(d["text"]) == d["label"] for d in holdout) return correct / len(holdout) print("Cheap model accuracy vs. strong-model labels:", accuracy(cheap_label)) # Cost delta (per the Module 10 pricing levers): # claude-opus-4-8 : $5.00 in / $25.00 out per 1M tokens # claude-haiku-4-5: $1.00 in / $5.00 out per 1M tokens # → ~5x cheaper per token AND lower latency. Across 5M calls/day, that delta is the # entire business case. The eval number tells you whether the quality is acceptable. The learning: distillation is only worth it if the eval says the cheap model is close enough and the cost/latency delta is large enough. Both numbers come from your existing harness — that’s why evals are the prerequisite. (c) Connect RFT to your eval grader . Sketch (no need to run) how the programmatic grader you built in Module 08 becomes a verifiable reward: . # Module 08 gave you something shaped like this — a grader that returns a score. def grade(model_output, example) -&gt; float: # e.g. did the JSON validate? did the unit test pass? exact-match the gold label? return 1.0 if is_correct(model_output, example) else 0.0 # In an RFT loop, that grader IS the reward signal: # 1. Sample N candidate outputs from the model for each training input. # 2. Score each with grade(...) ← your eval grader, unchanged. # 3. Update weights toward higher-scoring outputs. # No labeled "ideal output" required — only the ability to CHECK an output. The insight worth keeping: anywhere you can programmatically check an answer more easily than write the ideal one, you have a candidate reward function. Your eval harness isn’t just how you measure customization — it can be the thing you train against.

