Module 03 gave you a yes/no permission gate; Module 11 gave you the security rationale. Here we treat the gate as what it actually is: a product surface, not an afterthought. A bad approval prompt either gets rubber-stamped (useless) or exhausts the user (abandoned). A good one makes the right decision cheap and obvious. What a real approval prompt renders: . | The action — what the agent wants to do, in plain language, not raw JSON. | A preview / diff of the change — for an edit, the unified diff; for a bash, the exact command; for an API call, the request. Seeing the change is the entire trust transaction. An approval without a preview is a blind signature. | The blast radius — what this touches. “Modifies 1 file in your workspace” vs. “Deletes 4 rows from the production users table.” This is the reversibility/blast-radius signal from §3, surfaced to the human at the decision point. | . def render_approval(tu) -&gt; str: if tu.name == "edit": return (f"EDIT {tu.input['path']}
" f"blast radius: 1 file (workspace, reversible via git)

" f"{unified_diff(tu.input['path'], tu.input['new_text'])}") if tu.name == "bash": risky = gate(tu) return (f"BASH {tu.input['command']}
" f"blast radius: {'HIGH — irreversible / prod' if risky else 'low — workspace'}") return f"{tu.name} {json.dumps(tu.input, indent=2)}" . Beyond the single prompt, the surfaces that reduce friction without reducing safety: . | “Always allow X for this session.” After the user approves edit once, stop asking for edit this session. This directly fights alarm fatigue (§3). Scope it carefully — “always allow” should never silently cover the irreversible class. | Batching approvals. When the agent proposes five reversible edits, show them as one batch with five diffs and one approve button, not five sequential prompts. One decision, full visibility. | Plan mode / dry-run — the single highest-trust pattern. The agent proposes a complete step-by-step plan and waits for approval before executing anything. The human reviews the whole strategy up front, while it’s still free to change, rather than approving (or catching) actions one at a time after they’ve started. Plan mode front-loads the trust transaction to the cheapest possible moment. | Graduated autonomy levels — a user-facing dial. Expose the autonomy level as an explicit control the user sets per run: | . | Level | Behavior | . | read-only | Scout can read and analyze; every write/exec is gated. | . | auto-edit | Reversible edits auto-apply; irreversible/external actions gated. | . | full-auto | Everything runs; the human watches via the legibility surface and interrupts if needed. | . The dial is the user’s primary lever for spending trust as they earn it. New task in an unfamiliar area → read-only. Routine task they’ve watched succeed ten times → full-auto. The same Scout, the same model — the human just chose how much rope to give it. (Plan mode and auto-accept levels are exactly the pattern shipped by coding agents like Claude Code and Cursor — strong evidence this is the standard, not a one-off.) . AUTO_ALLOW = { "read-only": lambda tu: tu.name in ("read", "grep", "glob"), "auto-edit": lambda tu: tu.name in ("read", "grep", "glob", "edit") and not gate(tu), "full-auto": lambda tu: not gate(tu), # gate() still stops the irreversible class } def needs_approval(tu, level: str) -&gt; bool: return not AUTO_ALLOW[level](tu) . Notice gate() (the irreversible-class predicate from §3) is honored at every level including full-auto. The autonomy dial controls the reversible surface; the irreversible class is a hard floor. That’s the design rule: let the user dial up autonomy on what’s recoverable, never on what isn’t. 🛠️ Exercise (approval UX): Upgrade Scout’s gate to render action + diff/preview + blast radius, add session-level “always allow,” and wire the autonomy dial. Confirm that full-auto still stops a rm -rf via gate().
